<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mission civilisatrice</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The mission civilisatrice, or civilising mission, was the ideology used to justify French colonial rule. It claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had a duty to spread its language, culture and values to colonised peoples. It shaped education and policy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>, while masking exploitation and coercion.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the 18th century Europeans came to see history as a linear process of sociocultural evolution led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, and colonialists described non-Europeans as backward nations. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the philosopher Marquis de Condorcet set out a European holy duty to help peoples who, in his words, waited &quot;only to receive the means from us&quot; to civilise themselves. English colonial charters used similar language: the First Virginia Charter of 1606 spoke of bringing &quot;the infidels and savages&quot; to &quot;human civility&quot;.</p><p>According to the historian Jennifer Pitts, liberal thinkers of the 1780s such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremy-bentham">Jeremy Bentham</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Denis Diderot</a> were sceptical of empire. By the mid-19th century, however, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexis-de-tocqueville">Alexis de Tocqueville</a> endorsed empire on the basis of the civilising mission.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The French historian Raoul Girardet called the French ideology of civilising Africans colonial humanism. French colonists regarded the civilisations of the peoples they subjugated as backward and held colonisation to be good for them, and Jules Brévié, a former governor-general of French West Africa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>, presented the French as protectors of indigenous culture as well.</p><p>Other powers used the same reasoning. The British, who did not coin the term, invoked it above all where colonies paid little, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudyard-kipling">Rudyard Kipling</a>'s 1899 poem The White Man's Burden expressed it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> applied it after annexing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1898, with public health campaigns and a free public school system. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> tied it to its conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> to its rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><p>Portugal gave the idea legal form in the Estatuto do Indigenato of 1929, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar">António de Oliveira Salazar</a>. It separated colonial citizens under Portuguese law from the indigenas, governed through customary authorities, with a small group of assimilados between them who had some formal education and were not subjected to forced labour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Nineteenth-century elites of South American republics used the same rhetoric against indigenous peoples: when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> ended the occupation of Araucanía in 1883, President Domingo Santa María said the region had been subjugated by &quot;the moral and civilizing force of the republic&quot;. The historian Mary Renda argued that the American occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> from 1915 served economic exploitation and strategic advantage. Critics of development see modern programmes that require societies to give up their native cultures and become Western as a continuation of the civilising mission.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Civilizing mission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire was presented as a <b>mission civilisatrice</b>, which obscured a largely extractive relationship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
